\f1\b\fs36 \cf2 DC Motor Speed/Position Control with Smith Predictor \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs32 \cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 Task:
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Using the provided MathWorks example featuring a DC motor controlled by a four-quadrant chopper and the \cf2 \strokec2 Smith Predictor\cf2 \strokec2  structure (which includes a 5ms sensor delay), complete the following tasks:\
\pard\tx102\tx556\tx720\pardeftab720\li754\fi-498\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 a) 
\f1\b Motor Characterization:
\f0\b0  Analyze the motor's core parameters to determine the maximum continuous torque the motor can produce for stable speed control. Following this, calculate the motor's Torque Constant (Kt).\
b)
\f1\b  Open-Loop Performance:
\f0\b0  Obtain and plot the steady-state Speed-Torque Characteristic of the DC motor operating without the controller (i.e., operating solely from the supply voltage).\
c) 
\f1\b Position Control Design:
\f0\b0  Develop a position control system (output in radians) for the DC motor, ensuring the 5 ms sensor delay is incorporated into the system. Illustrate the resulting position response graphically.\
d)
\f1\b  Topology Comparison:
\f0\b0  Repeat the analyses and designs from parts a), b), and c) by modifying the model to represent a wound-field DC motor, including the necessary field winding dynamics and excitation.\
e) 
\f1\b Smith Predictor Analysis: 
\f0\b0 Explain the detailed block diagram structure of the Smith Predictor. Using Laplace domain transfer functions, analytically demonstrate how this predictor manages to nullify the destabilizing effect of the time delay (tau) on the closed-loop system's characteristic equation.\
